  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Mid November warm and dry spell likely ends \nSunday\n\nRidging at the surface and aloft loses its grip on the region by\nthe second half of the weekend - yielding to a potential \nsubtropical or tropical low lifting northward up the East Coast \nfollowed by a well defined cold frontal passage.\n\nThis anticipated pattern evolution will provide two more \nunseasonably mild and dry days today and Saturday (highs in the\n50s and 60s or +15-20F above the historical average) followed \nby rain most likely impacting the eastern portion of the \nforecast area Sunday into Sunday night. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cooler and potentially more active pattern into\nmonth-end\n\nWe have high confidence in a noticeable cooling trend for next \nweek with near to below average temps forecast from Tuesday \ninto the Thanksgiving holiday weekend. \n\nWhile there is still very high model variability next week in\nterms of the precip pattern, there appears to be a general \nconsensus toward a potentially more active setup with 2 more \ncoastal systems to contend with. The first system would arrive\nsometime Wednesday or Thanksgiving Day with a second over the \nlast weekend of November.",
